#' Differences from Python ftfy
#'
#' The package aims to return what the reference version returns: Python
#' ftfy 6.3.1 run on Python 3.13. A departure is a place where it
#' deliberately does something else, because R cannot do what the
#' reference version does or because what the reference version does
#' damages text. Every departure is recorded in the departure registry,
#' and this page lists all 7 of them. Each has the input that shows it,
#' what the reference version returns, and what this package returns or
#' the error it raises.
#'
#' @details
#' In the examples a string is written as Python and R both write it in
#' source: `\u00e9` is the character U+00E9. An output of the reference
#' version is shown as Python prints it, so `\xe9` is that same character
#' there.
#'
#' A departure is on by default, and no option brings the reference
#' version's result back.
#'
#' The registry is installed with the package, one file for each entry, in
#' the directory `system.file("departures", package = "ftfy")`. This page
#' is written from those files and is never edited by hand.
#'
#' Some other differences are not departures and are not listed here. They
#' are parts of Python ftfy that this package does not have, and things an
#' R function cannot return. See [ftfy][ftfy-package] for what is absent,
#' [ftfy_config()] for the options that are not taken, [ftfy_plan()] for
#' codec names, which have no aliases, and [apply_plan()] for a plan that
#' ends in bytes. Each function's help page has a section on its
#' differences from Python ftfy. A difference that is neither on this page
#' nor on those is a bug in this package: please report it.
#'
#' @name ftfy-differences
#' @aliases differences departures
#' @rawRd \section{apply_plan() replays normalize steps}{
#' \describe{
#' \item{Input}{
#' A plan that holds a normalize step, replayed with apply_plan().
#' fix_and_explain() records such a step whenever normalisation changes
#' the text, for example the plan 'normalize NFC' for 'e\\u0301'. None of
#' the upstream JSON cases gives a plan with this step.
#' }
#' \item{Reference version}{
#' An error. Run on ftfy 6.3.1 with Python 3.13:
#' fix_and_explain('e\\u0301') returns the text '\\xe9' with the plan
#' [('normalize', 'NFC')], and apply_plan('e\\u0301', [('normalize',
#' 'NFC')]) raises ValueError: Unknown plan step: normalize. So the
#' reference version cannot replay every plan it returns.
#' }
#' \item{This package}{
#' The step is replayed: the text is normalised in the form the step
#' names, NFC, NFD, NFKC or NFKD, with the Unicode data of the ICU library
#' that stringi was built with. apply_plan('e\\u0301', 'normalize NFC')
#' gives '\\u00e9'. Every plan the package returns can be replayed.
#' }
#' \item{Decided in}{
#' ADR 0002, \href{https://github.com/epabarker/r-ftfy/blob/main/docs/adr/0002-plans-are-a-vector-class-in-a-data-frame.md}{docs/adr/0002-plans-are-a-vector-class-in-a-data-frame.md}.
#' }
#' \item{Registry entry}{
#' \code{apply-plan-replays-normalize}
#' }
#' }
#' }
#' @rawRd \section{A decoded NUL is dropped}{
#' \describe{
#' \item{Input}{
#' Text that is repaired by decoding bytes with the codec utf-8-variants,
#' where the bytes hold C0 80, Java's encoding of U+0000. For example
#' 'null\\u00c0\\u20acseparated', whose characters U+00C0 U+20AC encode in
#' sloppy-windows-1252 to C0 80. Also a plan replayed with apply_plan()
#' whose step 'decode utf-8-variants' meets C0 80.
#' }
#' \item{Reference version}{
#' U+0000 in place of each C0 80. Run on ftfy 6.3.1 with Python 3.13:
#' b'null\\xc0\\x80separated'.decode('utf-8-variants') and
#' fix_encoding('null\\u00c0\\u20acseparated') both return
#' 'null\\x00separated'. fix_text() of the same text returns
#' 'nullseparated', because its fixer remove_control_chars then removes
#' the U+0000; fix_text() with remove_control_chars=False returns
#' 'null\\x00separated'. fix_and_explain() of it returns that text with
#' the plan [('encode', 'sloppy-windows-1252'), ('decode',
#' 'utf-8-variants'), ('apply', 'remove_control_chars')].
#' }
#' \item{This package}{
#' Nothing in place of each C0 80, because an R string cannot hold U+0000:
#' the decoder and fix_encoding() give 'nullseparated'. fix_text() with
#' default options gives the same text as the reference version, and with
#' remove_control_chars = FALSE it gives 'nullseparated' too. So does
#' fix_and_explain() with default options, but its plan is 'encode
#' sloppy-windows-1252 > decode utf-8-variants', without the step 'apply
#' remove_control_chars', because there is no U+0000 left for that fixer
#' to remove. The plan still replays to the text.
#' }
#' \item{Decided in}{
#' ADR 0007, \href{https://github.com/epabarker/r-ftfy/blob/main/docs/adr/0007-a-decoded-nul-is-dropped.md}{docs/adr/0007-a-decoded-nul-is-dropped.md}.
#' }
#' \item{Registry entry}{
#' \code{decoded-nul-dropped}
#' }
#' }
#' }
#' @rawRd \section{The options of the encoding repair are honoured by fix_text()}{
#' \describe{
#' \item{Input}{
#' Any text given to fix_text() with one of the options restore_byte_a0,
#' replace_lossy_sequences, decode_inconsistent_utf8 or fix_c1_controls
#' set to FALSE by name, where that option changes what the encoding
#' repair does. For example 'voil\\u00c3 le travail' (two spaces after the
#' first word) with restore_byte_a0, '\\u00c3\\ufffdtude' with
#' replace_lossy_sequences, '\\u2603 caf\\u00c3\\u00a9' with
#' decode_inconsistent_utf8 and '\\u0094\\u044f' with fix_c1_controls.
#' }
#' \item{Reference version}{
#' The option is not passed on to the encoding repair when it is given to
#' fix_text() as a keyword argument, so the encoding is repaired as with
#' default options. fix_text() then makes a config with explain=False
#' (ftfy/__init__.py:341), and fix_and_explain() calls fix_encoding(text)
#' with no config when it does not explain (ftfy/__init__.py:396). Run on
#' ftfy 6.3.1 with Python 3.13: fix_text('voil\\xc3 le travail',
#' restore_byte_a0=False) returns 'voil\\xe0 le travail' (one space),
#' fix_text('\\xc3\\ufffdtude', replace_lossy_sequences=False) returns
#' '\\ufffdtude', fix_text('\\u2603 caf\\xc3\\xa9',
#' decode_inconsistent_utf8=False) returns '\\u2603 caf\\xe9' and
#' fix_text('\\x94\\u044f', fix_c1_controls=False) returns '"\\u044f':
#' each is what fix_text() returns with no option. The option is honoured
#' when it comes in a config object, which explains unless told not to:
#' fix_text('voil\\xc3 le travail',
#' TextFixerConfig(restore_byte_a0=False)) returns 'voil\\xe0 le travail'
#' (both spaces), and the other three return '\\xc3\\ufffdtude', '\\u2603
#' caf\\xc3\\xa9' and '\\x94\\u044f'. fix_encoding() and
#' fix_encoding_and_explain() honour the option in both forms. So does
#' fix_and_explain() while it explains, which it does unless its config
#' says explain=False: fix_and_explain('voil\\xc3 le travail',
#' TextFixerConfig(explain=False, restore_byte_a0=False)) returns the text
#' 'voil\\xe0 le travail' (one space).
#' }
#' \item{This package}{
#' The option is honoured however it is given, by name or in a config made
#' by ftfy_config(), and fix_text() gives what the reference version gives
#' for the option in a TextFixerConfig: 'voil\\xe0 le travail' (both
#' spaces), '\\xc3\\ufffdtude', '\\u2603 caf\\xc3\\xa9' and '\\x94\\u044f'
#' for the four calls above. This package has no option explain and its
#' other functions give what the reference version gives while it
#' explains, so the difference is seen only in fix_text() with one of the
#' four options given by name. With default options nothing differs.
#' }
#' \item{Decided in}{
#' ADR 0001, \href{https://github.com/epabarker/r-ftfy/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-options-as-named-arguments-over-a-config-object.md}{docs/adr/0001-options-as-named-arguments-over-a-config-object.md}.
#' }
#' \item{Registry entry}{
#' \code{encoding-sub-options-honoured}
#' }
#' }
#' }
#' @rawRd \section{Invalid UTF-8 input is an error}{
#' \describe{
#' \item{Input}{
#' Any string whose bytes are not valid UTF-8 once it has been converted
#' by its mark, in a call to any exported function that takes text. For
#' example the bytes 63 61 66 E9 marked as UTF-8, the bytes ED A0 80 (a
#' lone surrogate, U+D800), or C0 80.
#' }
#' \item{Reference version}{
#' A Python string cannot hold arbitrary invalid bytes, so the nearest
#' inputs are strings holding surrogate code points, which the reference
#' version accepts. Run on ftfy 6.3.1 with Python 3.13:
#' fix_text('caf\\udce9') returns 'caf\\ufffd', fix_text('\\ud800')
#' returns '\\ufffd', and fix_encoding() returns both strings unchanged.
#' Bytes given in place of text, as in fix_text(b'caf\\xe9'), raise
#' UnicodeError.
#' }
#' \item{This package}{
#' An error raised by the entry step, before any other work. The message
#' says how many elements hold invalid UTF-8, names the positions of the
#' first five, and says that the data was probably read with the wrong
#' codec. No invalid input is repaired.
#' }
#' \item{Decided in}{
#' \url{https://github.com/epabarker/r-ftfy/issues/12#issuecomment-6858969101}.
#' }
#' \item{Registry entry}{
#' \code{invalid-utf8-input}
#' }
#' }
#' }
#' @rawRd \section{Normalisation follows the user's ICU}{
#' \describe{
#' \item{Input}{
#' Text that holds a character with a combining class or a decomposition
#' that one of the two Unicode versions has and the other does not: that
#' of the reference version, Unicode 15.1, and that of the ICU library
#' stringi was built with. It is normalised by fix_text() and
#' fix_and_explain() (the option normalization, in each of the forms NFC,
#' NFD, NFKC and NFKD) and by a normalize step replayed with apply_plan().
#' For example 'a\\U00010EFD\\u0301', where U+10EFD ARABIC SMALL LOW WORD
#' SAKTA was assigned in Unicode 15.0 with the combining class 220, and
#' '\\U0001E030', MODIFIER LETTER CYRILLIC SMALL A, assigned in Unicode
#' 15.0 with a compatibility decomposition.
#' }
#' \item{Reference version}{
#' The normalisation of Unicode 15.1. Run on ftfy 6.3.1 with Python 3.13:
#' fix_and_explain('a\\U00010EFD\\u0301') returns the text
#' '\\xe1\\U00010efd' with the plan [('normalize', 'NFC')], and
#' fix_and_explain('\\U0001E030', normalization='NFKC') returns '\\u0430'
#' with the plan [('normalize', 'NFKC')].
#' }
#' \item{This package}{
#' The normalisation of the user's ICU, whatever its version; the package
#' neither checks the version nor warns. With ICU 71.1, which has Unicode
#' 14.0 and does not know these characters, both texts come back unchanged
#' with the plan 'none'. With an ICU that has Unicode 15.0 or later, R
#' gives what the reference version gives. Text whose characters were all
#' assigned before both versions is normalised the same by both.
#' }
#' \item{Decided in}{
#' ADR 0005, \href{https://github.com/epabarker/r-ftfy/blob/main/docs/adr/0005-regex-and-unicode-data-are-generated-from-a-reference-python.md}{docs/adr/0005-regex-and-unicode-data-are-generated-from-a-reference-python.md}.
#' }
#' \item{Registry entry}{
#' \code{normalization-follows-icu}
#' }
#' }
#' }
#' @rawRd \section{A regex that overflows the backtracking limit is an error}{
#' \describe{
#' \item{Input}{
#' Text so long, and of such a shape, that matching one of the package's
#' patterns needs more than the 1e9 bytes of backtracking stack that every
#' regex call allows, in a call to any exported function. For example one
#' string of 50,000,000 characters that is U+00C3 U+00A9 repeated
#' 25,000,000 times, searched with UTF8_DETECTOR_RE (measured on stringi
#' 1.8.7 with ICU 71.1: 40,000,000 characters are matched and 50,000,000
#' overflow). Only input tens of millions of characters long, in one
#' string, gets near the limit.
#' }
#' \item{Reference version}{
#' Python's re has no backtracking limit. Run on ftfy 6.3.1 with Python
#' 3.13: chardata.UTF8_DETECTOR_RE.search('\\u00c3\\u00a9' * 25_000_000)
#' returns a match with span (0, 50000000), in under a second.
#' }
#' \item{This package}{
#' An error of class ftfy_regex_overflow. The message names the exported
#' function that was called, says how many strings were being matched and
#' the length in characters of the longest, and says to split the text
#' into shorter strings. No result is returned for any element of the
#' input.
#' }
#' \item{Decided in}{
#' ADR 0005, \href{https://github.com/epabarker/r-ftfy/blob/main/docs/adr/0005-regex-and-unicode-data-are-generated-from-a-reference-python.md}{docs/adr/0005-regex-and-unicode-data-are-generated-from-a-reference-python.md}.
#' }
#' \item{Registry entry}{
#' \code{regex-overflow}
#' }
#' }
#' }
#' @rawRd \section{A sequence cut short by a final newline is undecodable}{
#' \describe{
#' \item{Input}{
#' Bytes decoded with the codec utf-8-variants that end in C0 0A, or in
#' the first five bytes of a six-byte surrogate pair followed by 0A (ED, a
#' byte from A0 to AF, a byte from 80 to BF, ED, a byte from B0 to BF,
#' then 0A), for example ED A0 BD ED B8 0A. They are reached from text
#' that ends in a newline, such as 'sch\\u00c3\\u00b6n \\u00c0\\n' and
#' '\\u00ed\\u00a0\\u00bd\\u00ed\\u00b8\\n', and so from any such line
#' given to fix_text().
#' }
#' \item{Reference version}{
#' The newline is read as the missing last byte of the sequence and is
#' lost. Run on ftfy 6.3.1 with Python 3.13:
#' b'abc\\xc0\\n'.decode('utf-8-variants') returns 'abc\\x00' and
#' b'\\xed\\xa0\\xbd\\xed\\xb8\\n'.decode('utf-8-variants') returns
#' '\\U0001f60a'; with any byte after the newline, or without the newline,
#' both raise UnicodeDecodeError. fix_encoding('sch\\u00c3\\u00b6n
#' \\u00c0\\n') returns 'sch\\xf6n \\x00', fix_encoding_and_explain()
#' gives that text with the plan [('encode', 'latin-1'), ('decode',
#' 'utf-8-variants')], and fix_text() of it returns 'sch\\xf6n '.
#' fix_encoding('\\u00ed\\u00a0\\u00bd\\u00ed\\u00b8\\n') and fix_text()
#' of it return '\\U0001f60a'. In a text of several lines, a line that
#' ends this way is joined to the next: fix_text('one \\u00c3\\u00a9
#' \\u00c0\\ntwo \\u00c3\\u00a9') returns 'one \\xe9 two \\xe9'.
#' }
#' \item{This package}{
#' Decoding fails, as it does in the reference version when anything other
#' than a final newline follows, so this decoding repairs nothing and the
#' newline is kept. The repair then goes on as it does for any text that
#' does not decode: fix_encoding('sch\\u00c3\\u00b6n \\u00c0\\n') gives
#' 'sch\\xf6n \\xc0\\n', with the mojibake before the C0 repaired by the
#' fixer decode_inconsistent_utf8, so fix_encoding_and_explain() gives the
#' plan 'apply decode_inconsistent_utf8'.
#' fix_encoding('\\u00ed\\u00a0\\u00bd\\u00ed\\u00b8\\n') returns its
#' input unchanged, with the plan 'none'. fix_text() gives the same two
#' texts, and repairs every other line of its input as the reference
#' version does: fix_text('one \\u00c3\\u00a9 \\u00c0\\ntwo
#' \\u00c3\\u00a9') gives 'one \\xe9 \\xc0\\ntwo \\xe9', on two lines.
#' Replaying the reference version's plan for such a text with
#' apply_plan() is a failed replay, at the step decode utf-8-variants.
#' }
#' \item{Decided in}{
#' ADR 0004, \href{https://github.com/epabarker/r-ftfy/blob/main/docs/adr/0004-utf-8-variants-does-not-copy-upstreams-trailing-newline-behaviour.md}{docs/adr/0004-utf-8-variants-does-not-copy-upstreams-trailing-newline-behaviour.md}.
#' }
#' \item{Registry entry}{
#' \code{utf8-variants-trailing-newline}
#' }
#' }
#' }
NULL
